# SKETCH: draft rules for txt → md (pandoc beamer, --slide-level=3)
## Covers the commands used in linear.txt, plus a few neighbours
## Same syntax as usTests/test.format, with these additions (see notes at bottom)
##   %     one word         %+    the rest of the paragraph, without using it up
##   %|    up to the next |   NULLSLIDE   drop the whole slide (as now)

################################################################

# Specials

!SEP -{30,}

## Leading tabs become nested markdown items (depth n → 4(n-1) spaces + "- ")
## Commands can follow the tabs (POLL, ANS); their output becomes the item text
!TAB -

## The first paragraph of a slide with no command is its title
!TITLE DEFHEAD

################################################################

# Headings
## Pandoc makes # and ## into \section and \subsection, and ### into a frame
## so we no longer need NOFRAME, SLIDESTART, BSL or ESL

## UNIT is not defined in the current format files; it just shows up as a slide title
UNIT ### UNIT %%

SEC # %%
SS ## %%
SSS ## %%
TSEC # %%\n\n### %%
TSS ## %%\n\n### %%

DEFHEAD ### %%

## Paragraphs with no command
DEFCOMM %%

################################################################

# Slide variants (draft marks them; final and handouts would treat them differently)

PSLIDE ^DEFHEAD *%% (present)*
REPSLIDE ^DEFHEAD *%% (repeat)*
PRESLIDE ^DEFHEAD *%% (preview)*
CONT ^DEFHEAD *%%*
CSLIDE ^DEFHEAD *%%*
ASLIDE ^DEFHEAD [*%%*]{.uline}
QSLIDE ^DEFHEAD %%
EXTRA NULLSLIDE

################################################################

# Answers, comments, polls (spans: the Lua filter turns classes into LaTeX)

ANS \* [%%]{.answer}
NOANS \* [NOANS]{.answer}
COMMENT [*%%*]{.short}
CHANGE [**Change: %%**]{.change}
REMARK [**Remark: %%**]{.change}

## POLL label | question text: draft shows only the label
POLL [%|]{.poll} %!

## Notes-only and presentation-only paragraphs
NOTES %!
PRESENT ^%%

## Pandoc reads \href as a link; url comes first, so we can't use [text](url)
HREF \href{%}{%%}

################################################################

# Columns (fenced divs; pandoc writes the beamer columns)
## spans.lua puts \small in every column, as the old format did

BC :::: {.columns align=center}\n::: {.column width="54%"}
NC :::\n::: {.column width="40%"}
EC :::\n::::

BCC :::: {.columns align=center}\n::: {.column width="47%"}
NCC :::\n::: {.column width="47%"}
ECC ^EC

################################################################

# Figures
## Pandoc reads percent heights as fractions of \textheight and widths as fractions of \linewidth

FIG ![](%){height=90%}
HIGHFIG ![](%){height=100%}
WIDEFIG ![](%){width=100%}
SIDEFIG ^WIDEFIG %
CFIG ^WIDEFIG %

## Side by side. Old DBFIG set heights (\dfheight); this sets widths, so check tall images
DOUBLEFIG ![](%){width=48%}\hfill![](%){width=48%}
DOUBLEPDF ^DOUBLEFIG %+-0.pdf %-1.pdf

################################################################

# Pauses

PAUSE \*

################################################################

## Notes on what lect.py would need, compared with the usTests version
## * Split slides first, then paragraphs; check NULLSLIDE (and ENDTALK) per slide
## * Tab conversion, with commands allowed after the tabs
## * New patterns %, %+, %| (old lect.pl already has these meanings)
## * Pass raw LaTeX in DEFCOMM text through (\emph, \textbf and math are fine);
##   {\bf …} groups and the like need a sed pass, because pandoc escapes the braces
